From 3b7dbbeceb0e2d0fef8d58763c3241f145a1b1f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 19:39:20 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(world): preserve native Bedrock world clock semantics

Initialize the day clock from level time when StartGame has no day-cycle stop
time, preserving complete tick counts for day counts and moon phases.
Legacy SetTime follows the doDayLightCycle rule while game age advances
independently.

A saved official Windows Bedrock 1.26.51.1 session using protocol 2193 sends
SetTime 12445 followed by GameRulesChanged with dodaylightcycle=false.
Java previously retained the running rate from SetTime after storing that
rule update, so its lobby drifted into night while the native scene stayed
at twilight. The capture contains no SyncWorldClocks updates.

Track the legacy Overworld clock against game age. When the daylight rule
changes, resend its current full tick count and paused or running rate.
Resuming after a long pause must continue from the paused value rather than
resetting to the last server time or counting ticks spent paused. A new
legacy SetTime replaces the snapshot. Explicit Overworld SyncWorldClocks
updates clear legacy tracking and retain their own rates; updates to other
clocks remain independent.

Numerical tests cover long pauses, resume without a jump, new server times,
independent explicit clocks, and full-count packet serialization.
